/* =====================================================================
   PART 15 · PRINCIPAL ENGINEER THINKING: 8 scenarios
   Each: situation → first move (graded) → your plan → expert walkthrough
   (diagnose, options, decision, plan, risks, measures, what to tell
   leadership) → ADR that pre-fills the template.
   Company situations are fictional composites; public examples are labelled.
   ===================================================================== */
(function(){
const SDA = window.SDA;
const S = def => SDA.register(Object.assign({kind:'scenario'}, def));

/* 1 ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
S({id:'monolith-pain', icon:'🧱', title:'The monolith nobody can change', tags:['modular monolith', 'delivery', 'boundaries'],
  tagline:'120 engineers, weekly releases, 1 in 4 rolled back. Leadership wants a microservices rewrite.',
  situation:`<p>Your company’s main product is an eight-year-old monolith. It has grown to 120 engineers in 14 teams. Releases go out once a week on a “release train”, and about a quarter of them are rolled back. A change takes three weeks on average to reach production. The CTO has heard microservices will fix this and asks you to plan a rewrite into about 40 services.</p>`,
  facts:['Test suite: 90 minutes, about 8% flaky', 'Weekly release; ~25% rolled back', 'Median lead time merge → production: 3 weeks', 'One shared database with ~600 tables', 'Revenue is growing; features can’t stop'],
  before:{n:[['teams', 'client', '14 teams', '120 engineers', 0, 1], ['ci', 'service', 'CI', '90-min tests', 1, 0], ['mono', 'service', 'Monolith', 'weekly train', 2, 1], ['db', 'db', 'One database', '600 tables', 3, 1]], e:'teams>ci ci>mono teams>mono mono>db', f:[['Every change waits for the 90-minute test run and the weekly release train.', 'teams>ci:merge|ci>mono:weekly release'], ['Teams change the same code and tables, so one bad change rolls back everyone’s work.', 'teams>mono:14 teams, 1 deploy|mono>db']]},
  ask:'The CTO wants a rewrite plan by Friday. What is your first move?',
  choices:[
    ['Draft the 40-service architecture so the rewrite can start next quarter', 'poor', 'This answers the CTO’s solution, not the problem. Rewrites of large systems routinely take years, freeze features, and often recreate the same coupling over a network. You don’t yet know whether the pain is architectural at all.'],
    ['Measure where the pain actually comes from: pipeline time, rollbacks, which modules change together, who owns what', 'best', 'Yes. The symptoms (slow releases, rollbacks) can come from the delivery pipeline, test quality, missing ownership or coupling. A week of data (DORA metrics, git change coupling, incident causes) tells you which, and makes the conversation with the CTO about evidence.'],
    ['Freeze features for six months and refactor', 'poor', 'The business is growing and can’t stop shipping. A freeze also gives no guarantee of results and burns trust with product leadership.'],
    ['Split the code into two repositories to reduce merge conflicts', 'meh', 'It may help a little, but without understanding the coupling you might split along the wrong line and add cross-repository coordination.']],
  walk:{
    diagnose:['<b>Delivery metrics</b> (DORA): deploy frequency, lead time, change failure rate, time to restore. Where does the 3 weeks go: review, CI, the release train, or manual QA?', '<b>Change coupling</b> from a year of git history: which directories change together? Those are the real modules (or the real tangles).', '<b>Rollback causes</b>: test gaps, integration conflicts between teams, data migrations, configuration?', '<b>Ownership map</b>: which team owns which code and tables? Where do many teams edit the same files?', '<b>Runtime needs</b>: does any part need independent scaling or a different availability target?'],
    options:[['Big-bang rewrite into microservices', 'Clean slate; independent deploys eventually', 'Years of parallel work, frozen features, distributed-systems failure modes, likely to recreate coupling', 'No'], ['Extract everything with the strangler pattern', 'Incremental, keeps shipping', 'Still 40 services’ worth of operational overhead; extraction order unclear without boundaries', 'Not yet'], ['Modular monolith: enforce boundaries in one deployable, then extract selectively', 'Fast benefits, cheap boundary changes, no network failure modes', 'Needs tooling and discipline; one database remains for now', 'Yes'], ['Only fix CI and deployment', 'Quick wins in weeks', 'Leaves coupling and ownership problems', 'Yes, as step one']],
    decision:'Fix the delivery pipeline first (it is the cheapest and fastest win), then turn the monolith into a <b>modular monolith</b>: domains with owners, enforced dependency rules, and data access only through each module’s interface. Extract a module into a service only when it meets explicit criteria: it needs independent scaling or availability, or its team is blocked by others even with boundaries in place.',
    plan:[['Weeks 0–6', 'Parallelise tests, quarantine flaky ones, deploy daily behind feature flags, automate rollbacks', 'Daily deploys; lead time under 1 week'], ['Quarter 1–2', 'Define ~10 domains from change-coupling data; assign owners; add automated dependency checks in CI; route table access through module interfaces', 'Fewer than 10% of changes cross a module boundary'], ['Quarter 3+', 'Extract the 1–2 modules that meet the criteria (for example notifications, search) behind the strangler pattern', 'Extracted services deploy independently with their own SLOs'], ['Ongoing', 'Track DORA metrics per team; review boundaries quarterly', 'Trends improve for two quarters in a row']],
    risks:[['Boundary rules ignored under deadline pressure', 'Make violations fail CI; allow exceptions only with an ADR'], ['Extracted services become a distributed monolith', 'Extract only with clear criteria; events instead of chatty synchronous calls'], ['Leadership sees no “big change”', 'Report DORA metrics monthly; show lead time and rollback trends'], ['Engineers wanted microservices experience', 'Involve them in boundary design and the first extraction']],
    measure:['Deploy frequency: weekly → daily', 'Lead time: 3 weeks → under 2 days', 'Change failure rate: 25% → under 10%', 'Share of changes touching more than one module', 'Time for a new engineer to ship their first change'],
    say:'“The slowness comes mostly from our pipeline and from teams editing the same code, not from having one deployable. We can halve lead time in six weeks by fixing the pipeline, and get most of the independence of microservices within two quarters by enforcing boundaries inside the monolith, without a risky rewrite. We’ll split out services where the data shows a clear payoff.”',
    real:[{co:'Shopify', tier:'confirmed', text:'Shopify described keeping its Rails monolith and enforcing component boundaries inside it (a modular monolith) rather than rewriting into microservices.', src:'Shopify Engineering, “Deconstructing the Monolith” (2019)'}, {co:'DORA', tier:'confirmed', text:'The DORA research programme identified four key delivery metrics (deploy frequency, lead time, change failure rate, time to restore) that correlate with organisational performance.', src:'Forsgren, Humble & Kim, Accelerate (2018)'}]},
  after:{n:[['teams', 'client', 'Teams', 'own modules', 0, 1], ['ci', 'service', 'Pipeline', 'deploys daily', 1, 1], ['ord', 'service', 'Orders module', 'owned', 2, 0], ['cat', 'service', 'Catalog module', 'owned', 2, 1], ['pay', 'service', 'Payments module', 'owned', 2, 2], ['q', 'queue', 'Events', '', 3, 0], ['db', 'db', 'Database', 'schema per module', 3, 1], ['notif', 'service', 'Notifications', 'extracted', 4, 0]], e:'teams>ci ci>ord ci>cat ci>pay ord>db cat>db pay>db ord>q q>notif', f:[['Teams merge into a fast pipeline that deploys daily; CI rejects calls that break module boundaries.', 'teams>ci:merge (Orders team)|ci>ord:deploy'], ['Notifications was extracted because it scales differently; it consumes order events.', 'ord>q:order.placed|q>notif:send email']]},
  adr:{title:'Keep a modular monolith and extract services only on explicit criteria', context:'120 engineers in 14 teams share one monolith. Releases are weekly and ~25% are rolled back; lead time is 3 weeks. A rewrite into ~40 microservices was proposed. Data shows most delay comes from a 90-minute flaky test suite and the release train, and most rollbacks from changes crossing team areas.', drivers:'Cut lead time from 3 weeks to days\nKeep shipping features throughout\nReduce cross-team coupling\nAvoid adding distributed-systems failure modes without need', options:'Big-bang rewrite into microservices\nStrangler extraction of all domains\nModular monolith with enforced boundaries, selective extraction\nPipeline fixes only', decision:'Fix the delivery pipeline first, then enforce module boundaries and ownership inside the monolith. Extract a module only when it needs independent scaling or availability, or its team remains blocked by others.', positive:'Benefits within weeks, not years\nBoundaries make later extraction cheap\nNo new network failure modes for most of the system', negative:'One shared database for now\nBoundary enforcement needs tooling and discipline', revisit:'Lead time still above 3 days after two quarters, or a module meets the extraction criteria'},
  refs:['l:monolith-to-microservices', 'l:ddd-bounded-contexts', 'l:strangler-fig', 'l:ci-cd', 'l:hexagonal']});

/* 2 ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
S({id:'db-bottleneck', icon:'🗄️', title:'The database is the bottleneck', tags:['capacity', 'Postgres', 'scaling ladder'],
  tagline:'The primary runs at 85% CPU at peak and traffic grows 8% a month. The team wants to shard, or move to Cassandra.',
  situation:`<p>Your main PostgreSQL primary hits 85% CPU every evening. API p99 latency has crept from 300 ms to 1.8 s. Read replicas lag by up to 20 seconds at peak. Traffic grows about 8% a month. Two senior engineers propose sharding now; another wants to migrate to Cassandra “because it scales”.</p>`,
  facts:['Primary CPU 85% at peak, growing ~8% a month', 'API p99: 300 ms → 1.8 s in six months', '2 read replicas, lag up to 20 s at peak', '~1,500 application connections to the primary', 'Analytics dashboards query the primary directly'],
  ask:'What do you do first?',
  choices:[
    ['Start a migration to Cassandra', 'poor', 'A data model rewrite with new consistency semantics is a year of work and risk, and you don’t know yet what is consuming the database. Most relational workloads at this size are fixed far more cheaply.'],
    ['Begin sharding by customer id', 'poor', 'Sharding is the last rung of the ladder: it complicates every query, transaction and migration forever. It may be needed one day, but not before you know where the load comes from.'],
    ['Find out what the database is spending its time on: top queries, connections, cache hit rate, lock waits', 'best', 'Yes. Tools like pg_stat_statements usually show a handful of queries consuming most of the time: a missing index, an N+1 pattern, an unbounded report. Connection storms and analytics on the primary are other classic culprits.'],
    ['Move to a bigger instance', 'meh', 'A reasonable stopgap that buys a few months, and sometimes the right call. But on its own it postpones the real fix, and growth of 8% a month eats a 2× upgrade in about nine months.']],
  walk:{
    diagnose:['<b>Top queries by total time</b> (pg_stat_statements): usually a few account for most CPU', '<b>Index and plan problems</b>: sequential scans on large tables, N+1 patterns from the ORM', '<b>Connections</b>: 1,500 connections means 1,500 backend processes; pooling may cut CPU on its own', '<b>Workload mix</b>: how much is analytics, how much is reads that tolerate staleness, how much is writes', '<b>Locks, bloat and vacuum</b>: hot rows, long transactions, tables that need maintenance', '<b>Runway math</b>: at 8% monthly growth from 85%, you are already past a safe 70–80% target'],
    options:[['Query and index fixes', 'Cheapest, often the biggest win', 'Needs profiling time; one-off unless monitored', 'Do first'], ['Connection pooling (PgBouncer)', 'Cuts per-connection overhead and spikes', 'Transaction-mode pooling limits some session features', 'Do first'], ['Cache hot reads, use replicas for stale-tolerant reads', 'Removes read load from the primary', 'Staleness must be acceptable per query', 'Do next'], ['Move analytics to a warehouse via CDC', 'Removes heavy scans from OLTP', 'New pipeline to run', 'Do next'], ['Vertical scaling', 'Instant capacity', 'Cost; growth catches up', 'Stopgap'], ['Partition or archive big tables', 'Smaller indexes, faster vacuum', 'Schema work', 'If tables are huge'], ['Split by domain or shard', 'Horizontal write scaling', 'Permanent complexity in every query', 'Last, when write load demands it']],
    decision:'Climb the ladder in order of cost: fix the top queries and indexes, add connection pooling, serve stale-tolerant reads from cache and replicas, and move analytics off the primary with change-data capture. Upgrade the instance once as a safety margin while that work lands. Revisit sharding only if write load alone approaches the limit of the largest instance.',
    plan:[['Week 1', 'Profile; ship fixes for the top 5 queries; add PgBouncer; upgrade instance one size as a buffer', 'Peak CPU under 60%'], ['Weeks 2–6', 'Cache hot reads; route tolerant reads to replicas; move dashboards to a warehouse fed by CDC', 'Primary handles only OLTP; p99 back under 400 ms'], ['Quarter 2', 'Partition the largest tables by time; archive old rows', 'Vacuum and index sizes under control'], ['Ongoing', 'Capacity model with alerts at 60% and 70%; query review in code review', 'At least 6 months of runway at all times']],
    risks:[['Replica reads return stale data where it matters', 'Classify queries; read-your-writes paths stay on the primary'], ['Cache invalidation bugs', 'Cache-aside with TTLs; delete on write; start with rarely changing data'], ['Fixes regress as code changes', 'Track top queries on a dashboard; slow-query budget in CI or review'], ['Growth faster than expected', 'Runway model reviewed monthly; sharding design kept ready as a document']],
    measure:['Peak primary CPU (target under 60%)', 'API p99 back under 400 ms', 'Replica lag under 2 s', 'Months of runway at current growth (target ≥ 6)', 'Share of primary load that is analytics (target 0)'],
    say:'“We don’t need to change databases. A few queries and too many connections are doing most of the damage, and analytics shouldn’t run on the production database at all. Those fixes take weeks, not a year. If they cut load by 40%, CPU drops from 85% to about 51%, and at 8% monthly growth that gives roughly six months before we hit 80% again. We’ll use that time for the next steps and keep a sharding plan ready if writes ever need it.”',
    real:[{co:'Instagram', tier:'confirmed', text:'Instagram’s 2012 engineering post on sharding and IDs describes running sharded PostgreSQL only after growing on a simpler setup, with logical shards mapped to fewer physical servers to make later moves cheap.', src:'Instagram Engineering, “Sharding & IDs at Instagram” (2012)'}]},
  adr:{title:'Scale the primary database by optimisation and read offload before sharding', context:'The PostgreSQL primary peaks at 85% CPU with 8% monthly traffic growth; API p99 has risen to 1.8 s. Profiling shows the top 5 queries use ~55% of CPU, 1,500 direct connections, and analytics dashboards querying the primary.', drivers:'Restore p99 under 400 ms within 6 weeks\nKeep at least 6 months of capacity runway\nAvoid irreversible data-model changes', options:'Migrate to Cassandra\nShard PostgreSQL by customer\nOptimise queries, pool connections, offload reads and analytics, then reassess\nVertical scaling only', decision:'Optimise the top queries, add PgBouncer, move stale-tolerant reads to cache and replicas, move analytics to a warehouse via CDC, and upgrade the instance once as a buffer. Reassess sharding only if write load approaches the largest instance’s limit.', positive:'Weeks instead of a year\nNo change to the data model or consistency\nWarehouse unlocks better analytics', negative:'Replica reads are stale by seconds\nA new CDC pipeline to operate', revisit:'Peak write load exceeds 60% of the largest available instance, or runway falls under 6 months'},
  refs:['l:indexes', 'l:connection-pooling', 'l:replication', 'l:cache-strategies', 'l:oltp-olap', 'l:cdc', 'l:sharding', 'l:capacity-planning']});

/* 3 ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
S({id:'circular-dependency', icon:'🔁', title:'Two services that call each other', tags:['boundaries', 'events', 'coupling'],
  tagline:'Orders calls Inventory, and Inventory calls Orders. They deploy together and fail together.',
  situation:`<p>When an order is placed, the Orders service calls Inventory to reserve stock. To allocate stock, Inventory calls back into Orders to fetch order details, and when stock changes it calls Orders again to update order status. The two teams must coordinate every deploy, and when either service is slow, both time out. Last month a retry loop between them caused a 40-minute outage.</p>`,
  facts:['Orders → Inventory: reserve stock (synchronous)', 'Inventory → Orders: fetch order details during allocation (synchronous callback)', 'Inventory → Orders: update status on stock change (synchronous)', 'Deploys must be coordinated across both teams', 'One retry storm caused a 40-minute outage'],
  before:{n:[['c', 'client', 'Checkout', '', 0, 1], ['ord', 'service', 'Orders', '', 1, 1], ['odb', 'db', 'Orders DB', '', 1, 2], ['inv', 'service', 'Inventory', '', 3, 1], ['idb', 'db', 'Inventory DB', '', 3, 2]], e:'c>ord ord>inv~0.2 inv>ord~0.2 ord>odb inv>idb', f:[['Placing an order: Orders asks Inventory to reserve stock…', 'c>ord:place order|ord>inv:reserve SKU 42 ×2'], ['…and Inventory calls back into Orders for the order details. If either is slow, both are slow.', 'inv>ord:GET /orders/77 (callback)|ord>odb']]},
  ask:'What is your first move?',
  choices:[
    ['Merge the two services into one', 'meh', 'Sometimes right: if they are really one bounded context, merging is honest. But first check whether the cycle comes from a misplaced responsibility; merging hides that and grows a service two teams must share.'],
    ['List each call: what data or decision it needs, who should own it, and whether the caller must wait', 'best', 'Yes. Cycles usually mean a responsibility is in the wrong place, or data is being fetched that could have been sent. Once each call is justified (or not), the fix is usually obvious.'],
    ['Add circuit breakers in both directions', 'meh', 'Breakers limit the damage of a slow dependency, and you should have them anyway. But they treat the symptom: the coupling, coordinated deploys and cycle remain.'],
    ['Let both services read and write a shared database instead of calling each other', 'poor', 'This removes visible calls by hiding the coupling in shared tables. Now neither team can change its schema safely, which is worse.']],
  walk:{
    diagnose:['Draw every call between the two with its <b>purpose</b>, <b>data</b> and whether the caller <b>must wait</b> for the answer', 'Ask who owns each concept: an order’s status belongs to Orders; stock levels belong to Inventory', 'Find the callback’s purpose: Inventory needs line items it could have received in the reserve request', 'Find the status update’s purpose: Orders needs to react to stock changes, which is a fact Inventory can publish', 'Check whether the two are really one domain (same team, same changes, same data)'],
    options:[['Merge into one service', 'No network cycle; one deploy', 'Bigger service shared by two teams; hides the ownership question', 'Only if it is one domain'], ['Send the needed data in the request', 'Removes the callback; Inventory needs no knowledge of Orders', 'Request payload grows slightly', 'Yes'], ['Replace the reverse calls with events', 'Dependencies point one way; Orders reacts asynchronously', 'Eventual consistency of order status; event infrastructure', 'Yes'], ['Extract a third “allocation” service', 'Can be right if allocation is its own domain', 'One more service; risk of a new cycle', 'Not yet']],
    decision:'Make the dependency point one way. Orders calls Inventory synchronously to reserve stock and sends all the data Inventory needs (line items, quantities, order id), so no callback is required. Inventory publishes events (StockReserved, StockReleased, OutOfStock) through an outbox, and Orders consumes them to update the order status it owns. A CI check fails the build if a dependency cycle reappears.',
    plan:[['Week 1', 'Map the calls; agree ownership; write the ADR with both teams', 'Both teams sign off'], ['Weeks 2–3', 'Extend the reserve request with line items; stop the callback behind a flag', 'Callback traffic falls to 0'], ['Weeks 3–6', 'Publish Inventory events through an outbox; Orders consumes them; run both paths and compare', 'Event-driven status matches the old path for 2 weeks'], ['Week 7', 'Remove the old synchronous status call; add a dependency-cycle check to CI', 'Independent deploys']],
    risks:[['Order status lags behind stock changes', 'Show “confirming” in the UI; events usually arrive within seconds'], ['Events lost or duplicated', 'Outbox for publishing; idempotent consumer keyed by event id'], ['Teams drift back into callbacks', 'Automated dependency-graph check in CI'], ['Payload changes break consumers', 'Versioned event schemas']],
    measure:['Synchronous calls Inventory → Orders: 0', 'Deploys requiring cross-team coordination: 0', 'Incidents involving both services', 'p99 of order placement'],
    say:'“The two services are tangled because Inventory asks Orders for data it could have been given, and tells Orders things by calling it instead of announcing them. We’ll make the calls go one way and use events for the rest. In about six weeks the teams deploy independently, and a slow Inventory can no longer take Orders down with it.”'},
  after:{n:[['c', 'client', 'Checkout', '', 0, 1], ['ord', 'service', 'Orders', 'owns status', 1, 1], ['odb', 'db', 'Orders DB', '', 1, 2], ['ev', 'queue', 'Stock events', 'outbox', 2, 0], ['inv', 'service', 'Inventory', 'owns stock', 3, 1], ['idb', 'db', 'Inventory DB', '', 3, 2]], e:'c>ord ord>inv ord>odb inv>idb inv>ev ev>ord', f:[['Orders sends everything Inventory needs in the reserve call: no callback.', 'c>ord:place order|ord>inv:reserve {sku, qty, order_id}'], ['Stock changes are published as events; Orders updates the status it owns.', 'inv>idb|inv>ev:StockReserved|ev>ord:update order status|ord>odb']]},
  adr:{title:'Inventory publishes events instead of calling Orders', context:'Orders and Inventory call each other synchronously (reserve, a callback for order details, and status updates). Deploys must be coordinated and a retry loop between them caused a 40-minute outage.', drivers:'Independent deploys for both teams\nA slow Inventory must not take Orders down\nClear ownership of order status and stock', options:'Merge the services\nSend required data in requests and publish events for the reverse direction\nExtract a third allocation service', decision:'Orders → Inventory remains synchronous for reservations and carries all needed data. Inventory publishes StockReserved / StockReleased / OutOfStock events through an outbox; Orders consumes them to update order status. A CI check forbids dependency cycles.', positive:'One-way dependency; independent deploys\nNo retry storms between the two\nOwnership is explicit', negative:'Order status is eventually consistent (seconds)\nEvent infrastructure and idempotent consumers required', revisit:'If allocation logic grows into its own domain with separate ownership'},
  refs:['l:ddd-bounded-contexts', 'l:event-driven', 'l:saga-outbox', 'l:circuit-breaker', 'l:api-composition']});

/* 4 ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
S({id:'cloud-bill', icon:'💸', title:'The cloud bill doubled', tags:['FinOps', 'unit cost', 'right-sizing'],
  tagline:'From $400K to $800K a month in six months, while revenue grew 15%. Finance wants 30% off within a quarter.',
  situation:`<p>Your monthly cloud bill went from $400K to $800K in six months. Revenue grew 15% in the same period. The CFO asks engineering to cut the bill by 30% this quarter and suggests every team cut its spending by 30%.</p>`,
  facts:['Bill $400K → $800K per month in 6 months', 'Revenue +15% in the same period', 'Only ~40% of resources are tagged with an owner', 'Average CPU utilisation across compute: ~12%', 'A new analytics feature launched four months ago', 'No savings plans or reserved capacity yet'],
  ask:'What is your first move?',
  choices:[
    ['Ask every team to cut 30%', 'poor', 'Flat cuts punish efficient teams and barely touch the real driver, which is usually concentrated in a few line items. Teams also start cutting things that matter (redundancy, backups).'],
    ['Buy three-year commitments for all current usage to get the discount', 'meh', 'Commitments save real money, but buying them before right-sizing locks in the waste for years. Buy them for the baseline that remains after cleanup.'],
    ['Make cost visible: allocate spend to services and teams, find the top drivers, and compute unit cost', 'best', 'Yes. Cost doubled while revenue grew 15%, so something specific changed. Breaking the bill down by service, team and line item (compute, storage, data transfer, logging) usually shows a handful of causes.'],
    ['Plan a migration to our own data centre', 'poor', 'A multi-year project with large up-front cost doesn’t meet a one-quarter goal, and you don’t yet know whether the problem is the platform or how it’s used.']],
  walk:{
    diagnose:['<b>Allocation</b>: tag coverage from 40% → 90%; cost per service and per team', '<b>Top line items and their growth</b>: compute, storage, data transfer (cross-zone, NAT gateways, egress), logging and metrics ingestion, managed databases', '<b>Unit cost</b>: cost per order or per active user, month by month. It should fall as you grow; here it nearly doubled', '<b>Utilisation</b>: 12% average CPU suggests over-provisioning and idle environments', '<b>What changed</b>: the analytics launch, new log levels, retention changes, a chatty service across zones'],
    options:[['Flat cut per team', 'Simple to announce', 'Ignores where waste is; harms reliability', 'No'], ['Clean-up and right-sizing', 'Fast, low risk', 'Needs owners for each resource', 'Yes, first'], ['Architecture fixes (data transfer, caching, data tiering, log sampling)', 'Removes structural waste', 'Engineering time', 'Yes, targeted'], ['Commitments (savings plans, reserved capacity)', 'Large discount on steady usage', 'Locks in a baseline', 'Yes, after right-sizing'], ['Leave the cloud', 'Possibly cheaper for stable workloads at scale', 'Years of work; not a quarterly fix', 'Not now']],
    decision:'Treat cost as an engineering metric. In the first two weeks, allocate every dollar to an owner and publish unit cost. Then attack the biggest drivers: delete orphaned resources, right-size over-provisioned compute, schedule non-production environments off at night, reduce log volume and retention, fix cross-zone and NAT data transfer, and tier cold storage. Buy savings plans only for the baseline that remains. Add budgets and anomaly alerts per team so this doesn’t silently happen again.',
    plan:[['Weeks 1–2', 'Tagging to 90%; cost dashboards per team; unit cost per order', 'Every major cost has an owner'], ['Weeks 2–6', 'Quick wins: orphaned volumes and snapshots, idle load balancers, dev environments off nights and weekends, log retention 30 → 7 days for debug logs', '~10–12% saved (illustrative)'], ['Weeks 4–10', 'Right-size compute from utilisation data; autoscaling for the analytics jobs; cross-zone traffic and NAT fixes; storage lifecycle rules', 'A further ~10% (illustrative)'], ['Weeks 8–12', 'Savings plans for the remaining steady baseline', 'A further ~8–10% (illustrative)'], ['Ongoing', 'Budgets and anomaly alerts per team; cost estimate in design reviews', 'Unit cost trends down']],
    risks:[['Right-sizing hurts peak performance', 'Use p95 utilisation over a month, not averages; change gradually with autoscaling'], ['Log reduction hides what you need in incidents', 'Sample debug logs; keep errors and audit logs in full'], ['Commitments bought for workloads that later shrink', 'Commit to 60–70% of the steady baseline; use flexible plan types'], ['Teams see cost work as a distraction', 'Show their unit cost; make it part of normal reviews, not a crusade']],
    measure:['Monthly bill and unit cost (per order, per active user)', 'Tag coverage', 'Average and p95 utilisation of compute', 'Data transfer and logging as a share of the bill', 'Anomalies caught by alerts before month end'],
    say:'“Our bill doubled mainly because of a few things we can name: idle and oversized servers, a new analytics feature, logs, and data moving between zones. Flat cuts would hurt reliability and miss those. We’ll show every team what they spend and what it costs per order, fix the big items first, and only then lock in discounts. A 30% cut this quarter is realistic, and the unit cost dashboard keeps it from creeping back.”',
    real:[{co:'Amazon Prime Video', tier:'confirmed', text:'The Prime Video team described cutting a monitoring service’s infrastructure cost by over 90% by consolidating distributed serverless components into a single process.', src:'Prime Video Tech blog (2023)'}, {co:'FinOps Foundation', tier:'confirmed', text:'The FinOps framework organises cloud cost management into inform (visibility and allocation), optimise, and operate phases.', src:'FinOps Foundation framework'}]},
  adr:{title:'Manage cloud cost by unit cost and ownership; commit only after right-sizing', context:'The cloud bill doubled to $800K/month in six months while revenue grew 15%. Only 40% of resources were tagged; average CPU utilisation is ~12%. Finance requested a 30% reduction within a quarter.', drivers:'30% reduction this quarter without hurting reliability\nStop silent cost growth\nCost visible to the teams that cause it', options:'Flat 30% cut per team\nClean-up, right-sizing and targeted architecture fixes, then commitments\nCommit to all current usage immediately\nMove to own data centres', decision:'Allocate all spend to owners and track unit cost; remove waste and right-size first; fix structural drivers (data transfer, logging, storage tiers); buy savings plans for the remaining baseline; add per-team budgets and anomaly alerts.', positive:'Targets the real drivers\nUnit cost becomes a tracked engineering metric\nDiscounts apply to a right-sized baseline', negative:'Engineering time diverted for a quarter\nCommitments reduce flexibility', revisit:'Unit cost rises for two consecutive months'},
  refs:['l:finops', 'l:autoscaling', 'l:capacity-planning', 'l:storage-types', 'l:logs-metrics-traces']});

/* 5 ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
S({id:'cascading-failures', icon:'🌊', title:'Cascading failures every few weeks', tags:['resilience', 'timeouts', 'retries'],
  tagline:'A slow recommendation service keeps taking down checkout. Every layer retries three times.',
  situation:`<p>Your e-commerce platform has about 30 services. Twice a month, an incident starts in a non-critical service (recommendations, reviews, loyalty points) and ends with checkout failing. The latest one: recommendations slowed to an 8-second p99 after a deploy, checkout’s request threads filled up waiting for it, and payments started timing out. The team wants to add more instances everywhere.</p>`,
  facts:['~30 services; 2 major incidents a month', 'Gateway, checkout and payments each retry failed calls 3 times', 'Default HTTP client timeout: 30 s', 'Checkout uses one shared thread pool for all downstream calls', 'Recommendations is not needed to complete a purchase'],
  before:{n:[['u', 'client', 'Shoppers', '', 0, 1], ['gw', 'lb', 'Gateway', 'retry ×3', 1, 1], ['co', 'service', 'Checkout', 'one thread pool', 2, 1], ['recs', 'service', 'Recommendations', 'p99 8 s', 3, 0], ['pay', 'service', 'Payments', 'retry ×3', 3, 2], ['psp', 'ext', 'Bank / PSP', '', 4, 2]], e:'u>gw gw>co co>recs co>pay pay>psp', f:[['Recommendations slows down; checkout threads wait up to 30 s, and every layer retries.', 'u>gw:checkout|gw>co|co>recs:waits, retries ×3'], ['The shared pool is full, so payment calls can’t even start: a non-critical service has taken down checkout.', 'co>pay:no free threads|gw>u:503']]},
  ask:'What is your first move?',
  choices:[
    ['Add more instances of every service', 'poor', 'More instances of a service blocked on a slow dependency just means more blocked threads. Extra capacity can even make retry storms bigger.'],
    ['Study the recent incidents: map the failure chain, timeouts, retries and thread pools at each hop', 'best', 'Yes. Cascades follow a pattern: long timeouts, retries at every layer, shared resources, and no fallback for non-critical calls. The incident timelines will show exactly which of these turned a slow service into an outage.'],
    ['Rewrite the recommendation service so it is never slow', 'meh', 'Improving it is fine, but some dependency will always be slow eventually. The system must survive that; one fast service doesn’t fix the architecture.'],
    ['Adopt a service mesh', 'meh', 'A mesh can enforce timeouts, retries and breakers consistently, which helps. But it is a big platform change, and without decided policies it just moves the same defaults somewhere else.']],
  walk:{
    diagnose:['<b>Retry amplification</b>: three layers each retrying 3 times means up to 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 attempts per user request (81 with a fourth layer) hitting a struggling service', '<b>Timeouts</b>: a 30 s default means a slow dependency holds a thread for 30 s; callers’ timeouts are often shorter than callees’, so work continues after nobody is waiting', '<b>Shared resources</b>: one thread or connection pool for all dependencies lets the slowest one starve the rest', '<b>Critical path</b>: which calls are needed to take money? Recommendations, reviews and loyalty are not', '<b>Detection</b>: how long until anyone noticed, and could the system have shed load by itself?'],
    options:[['More capacity', 'Simple', 'Doesn’t stop blocking or retry storms', 'No'], ['Timeouts with deadline propagation', 'Bounded waiting; no work for callers who gave up', 'Needs tuning per call', 'Yes'], ['Retry at one layer only, with budgets and jitter', 'Removes amplification', 'Some transient errors surface sooner', 'Yes'], ['Circuit breakers + fallbacks for non-critical calls', 'Checkout works without recommendations', 'Degraded experience during incidents', 'Yes'], ['Bulkheads (separate pools per dependency)', 'One slow dependency can’t starve others', 'More pools to size', 'Yes'], ['Load shedding at the edge', 'Protects the core under overload', 'Some requests rejected early', 'Yes']],
    decision:'Adopt a resilience standard for every service: (1) explicit timeouts derived from a request deadline passed downstream; (2) retries at one layer only, with exponential backoff, jitter and a retry budget; (3) circuit breakers with fallbacks for every non-critical dependency (checkout shows popular items when recommendations are down); (4) separate pools per dependency; (5) load shedding at the gateway. Verify with game days that inject latency into each non-critical service.',
    plan:[['Weeks 1–2', 'Classify every dependency as critical or non-critical; set timeouts on the checkout path; remove retries from all but one layer', 'No retry multiplication on the checkout path'], ['Weeks 2–6', 'Breakers and fallbacks for recommendations, reviews, loyalty; separate pools in checkout', 'Checkout succeeds with any non-critical service down (tested)'], ['Weeks 6–10', 'Deadline propagation library; gateway load shedding; shared defaults in the service template', '90% of services use the standard'], ['Ongoing', 'Monthly game day injecting latency; resilience checklist in design reviews', 'Incidents fall; blast radius shrinks']],
    risks:[['Timeouts set too low cause false failures', 'Base them on measured p99.9 plus margin; adjust from data'], ['Fallbacks rot because they are rarely used', 'Exercise them in game days and in a small share of production traffic'], ['Teams skip the standard', 'Put defaults in the shared client library and service template'], ['Breakers open on healthy services', 'Use error-rate and latency thresholds with minimum request counts']],
    measure:['Incidents per month where a non-critical service affected checkout (target 0)', 'Checkout success rate during dependency failures', 'Retry amplification on critical paths (target ≤ 1.1×)', 'Time to detect and time to recover', 'Game days passed'],
    say:'“Our outages aren’t caused by one bad service; they’re caused by how services wait for each other. Every layer retries, waits up to 30 seconds and shares one pool, so any slow service can take checkout down. We’ll set short timeouts, retry in one place only, and let checkout work without the nice-to-have services. We’ll prove it monthly by deliberately slowing those services down.”',
    real:[{co:'Netflix', tier:'confirmed', text:'Netflix open-sourced Hystrix (circuit breakers, bulkheads and fallbacks) and Chaos Monkey, and has written extensively about designing for dependency failure.', src:'Netflix Technology Blog'}, {co:'AWS', tier:'confirmed', text:'The Amazon Builders’ Library article on timeouts, retries and backoff with jitter recommends retrying at a single layer and using retry budgets to avoid amplification.', src:'Amazon Builders’ Library, “Timeouts, retries, and backoff with jitter”'}]},
  after:{n:[['u', 'client', 'Shoppers', '', 0, 1], ['gw', 'lb', 'Gateway', 'deadline 2 s, shed', 1, 1], ['fb', 'cache', 'Fallback', 'popular items', 2, 0], ['co', 'service', 'Checkout', 'pool per dependency', 2, 1], ['recs', 'service', 'Recommendations', 'breaker: open', 3, 0], ['pay', 'service', 'Payments', 'only retry layer', 3, 2], ['psp', 'ext', 'Bank / PSP', '', 4, 2]], e:'u>gw gw>co co>recs co>fb co>pay pay>psp', f:[['Recommendations is slow, so its breaker opens; checkout shows the fallback list instantly.', 'u>gw:checkout|gw>co|co>recs:breaker open, skip|co>fb:popular items'], ['Payments has its own pool and deadline, so it is unaffected.', 'co>pay:own pool|pay>psp:charge']]},
  adr:{title:'Resilience standard: deadlines, single-layer retries, breakers and bulkheads', context:'Non-critical services (recommendations, reviews, loyalty) cause checkout outages about twice a month. Three layers each retry 3×, client timeouts default to 30 s, and checkout uses one shared pool.', drivers:'Checkout must succeed when any non-critical service is down or slow\nStop retry amplification\nConsistent behaviour across ~30 services', options:'Add capacity\nService mesh first\nShared resilience standard in client libraries (deadlines, retries, breakers, bulkheads, shedding)\nRewrite slow services', decision:'Every remote call gets a timeout derived from a propagated deadline. Only the layer closest to the dependency retries, with backoff, jitter and a retry budget. Non-critical dependencies get breakers and fallbacks; each dependency gets its own pool; the gateway sheds load. Verified with monthly latency-injection game days.', positive:'Failures stay contained\nPredictable latency under stress\nDefaults live in the service template', negative:'Degraded pages during incidents\nTimeouts need tuning and review', revisit:'If a mesh is adopted, move the policies into it unchanged'},
  refs:['l:timeouts-retries', 'l:circuit-breaker', 'l:bulkhead-fallback', 'l:load-shedding', 'l:chaos-engineering', 'pg:retry-jitter', 'pg:circuit-breaker', 'pg:chaos-monkey']});

/* 6 ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
S({id:'microservices-hype', icon:'🎈', title:'Microservices without a business need', tags:['simplicity', 'YAGNI', 'influence'],
  tagline:'Twelve engineers, one product, a monolith that works. A new senior hire proposes Kubernetes, Kafka and 15 services.',
  situation:`<p>You lead architecture at a startup with 12 engineers. The product is a single web app with about 20K daily users, a Django monolith and one PostgreSQL database. Deploys happen several times a day and incidents are rare. A respected new senior engineer has proposed moving to 15 microservices on Kubernetes with Kafka between them, “so we’re ready to scale”. Several engineers are excited.</p>`,
  facts:['12 engineers in 2 teams', '~20K daily active users, growing ~5% a month', 'Deploys several times a day; incidents rare', 'No part of the system has unusual scaling needs', 'The team has no Kubernetes or Kafka operations experience'],
  ask:'How do you respond?',
  choices:[
    ['Approve it: the team is excited and it will help hiring', 'poor', 'Excitement is a real factor, but you’d be paying permanently for distributed-systems complexity (network failures, distributed data, observability, on-call for new infrastructure) without a problem it solves. Twelve engineers would spend much of their time running the platform.'],
    ['Reject it firmly in the meeting', 'meh', 'Probably the right outcome, but rejecting without engaging loses a strong engineer’s trust and misses whatever real pain prompted the proposal.'],
    ['Ask what problem it solves, what evidence shows that problem, and what it would cost: then decide together', 'best', 'Yes. Make the proposer state the problem (“deploys conflict?”, “one part needs to scale?”) and check it against data. If there is no problem today, agree on the specific signals that would justify revisiting. You keep the person and make a good decision.'],
    ['Run a pilot by building the next feature as five microservices', 'poor', 'A pilot without a problem to solve proves only that microservices can be built. It also leaves permanent services in production that someone has to run.']],
  walk:{
    diagnose:['What problem does the proposal solve <b>today</b>? Are deploys blocked, teams stepping on each other, a component needing independent scaling?', 'What do microservices <b>cost</b>: network failure handling, distributed transactions, cross-service debugging, tracing, platform operations, on-call', 'Team size: microservices mainly help when many teams need to deploy independently; 2 teams rarely do', 'Growth: at 5% a month, 20K DAU becomes ~36K in a year; a well-run monolith handles far more', 'The real motivation: learning, hiring, worry about the future? Each has a better answer'],
    options:[['Adopt 15 microservices + Kubernetes + Kafka', 'Learning; theoretical future scaling', 'Large permanent ops cost; slower delivery now', 'No'], ['Keep the monolith as is', 'Zero migration cost', 'May drift into a tangled codebase', 'Partly'], ['Modular monolith with clear boundaries + agreed extraction triggers', 'Keeps speed; makes future extraction cheap', 'Requires discipline', 'Yes'], ['Extract one genuinely independent workload (e.g. a background job runner)', 'Real learning on a real need', 'Only if such a workload exists', 'If justified']],
    decision:'Keep a well-structured monolith. Invest in module boundaries inside it, in CI/CD and in observability, which pay off now and make any future extraction cheap. Write down, with the proposer, the triggers that would reopen the decision: more than ~5 teams blocking each other on deploys, a component whose scaling or availability needs clearly differ, or a compliance requirement to isolate data. Give the engineer ownership of the boundary work and of the trigger dashboard.',
    plan:[['This week', 'One-to-one with the proposer; then a short written comparison (problem, costs, benefits, triggers)', 'Shared understanding, not a vote'], ['Month 1', 'Define module boundaries in the codebase; add dependency checks to CI', 'Boundaries enforced'], ['Months 1–3', 'Improve tracing and dashboards; tidy deploy pipeline', 'Any request traceable end to end'], ['Every quarter', 'Review the triggers with data', 'Decision revisited on evidence']],
    risks:[['The proposer feels dismissed and leaves', 'Involve them in writing the ADR and owning the boundary work'], ['Monolith drifts into a tangle anyway', 'Enforced boundaries, ownership, regular refactoring time'], ['A real scaling need appears suddenly', 'Triggers reviewed quarterly; boundaries keep extraction cheap']],
    measure:['Lead time and deploy frequency stay high', 'Boundary violations in CI (target 0)', 'Trigger metrics: teams blocked on deploys, component load profiles', 'Engineer satisfaction in the next survey'],
    say:'“Microservices solve problems we don’t have yet: many teams blocking each other, or parts of the system that need to scale very differently. Adopting them now would slow all 12 of us down to run infrastructure. We’ll keep one deployable, organise it into clean modules so splitting is easy later, and we’ve written down exactly what would make us split.”',
    real:[{co:'Segment', tier:'confirmed', text:'Segment’s engineering post “Goodbye Microservices” described consolidating over 100 destination services back into a single service after operational overhead outweighed the benefits.', src:'Segment Engineering blog (2018)'}, {co:'Amazon Prime Video', tier:'confirmed', text:'The Prime Video team moved a monitoring workload from distributed components to a single process, reporting over 90% lower infrastructure cost.', src:'Prime Video Tech blog (2023)'}]},
  adr:{title:'Keep a modular monolith; extract services only on agreed triggers', context:'12 engineers in 2 teams run a Django monolith serving ~20K DAU with several deploys a day and rare incidents. A proposal to move to 15 microservices on Kubernetes with Kafka was made to prepare for scale. No component has distinct scaling needs and the team lacks experience operating those platforms.', drivers:'Keep delivery speed\nAvoid operational load the team can’t carry\nMake future extraction cheap if needed', options:'15 microservices on Kubernetes with Kafka\nMonolith unchanged\nModular monolith with enforced boundaries and extraction triggers', decision:'Keep the monolith, enforce module boundaries in CI, and improve observability and deployment. Revisit when any trigger is met: more than ~5 teams blocking each other on deploys, a component with clearly different scaling or availability needs, or a data-isolation requirement.', positive:'Team time goes to the product\nSimpler operations and debugging\nBoundaries prepare for later extraction', negative:'Less hands-on distributed-systems experience for the team\nBoundaries need ongoing discipline', revisit:'Quarterly review of the triggers'},
  refs:['l:monolith-to-microservices', 'l:kiss-yagni-soc', 'l:tradeoff-thinking', 'l:kubernetes-basics', 'l:hexagonal']});

/* 7 ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
S({id:'multi-region', icon:'🌍', title:'“We need multi-region”', tags:['disaster recovery', 'RTO / RPO', 'replication'],
  tagline:'After a four-hour regional outage, the CEO wants active-active in every region by next quarter.',
  situation:`<p>Your cloud region had a four-hour outage last week and your e-commerce site was down the entire time. The CEO tells you: “We need to be multi-region, active-active, by next quarter.” Today everything runs in one region across three availability zones, with a single PostgreSQL primary and nightly backups stored in the same region.</p>`,
  facts:['One region, three availability zones', 'PostgreSQL primary with a same-region standby', 'Nightly backups, stored in the same region', 'SLO 99.9% (≈ 8.8 hours of downtime a year)', 'The outage used about 46% of the yearly error budget in one go', 'Checkout writes must never be lost; browsing can be slightly stale'],
  before:{n:[['u', 'client', 'Shoppers', '', 0, 1], ['dns', 'lb', 'DNS / LB', '', 1, 1], ['app', 'service', 'App', 'Mumbai, 3 AZs', 2, 1], ['db', 'db', 'Postgres primary', 'Mumbai', 3, 1], ['bk', 'db', 'Backups', 'same region', 3, 2]], e:'u>dns dns>app app>db db>bk', f:[['Everything runs in one region; when the region fails, the site is down until it recovers.', 'u>dns|dns>app|app>db']]},
  ask:'What is your first move?',
  choices:[
    ['Start building active-active for every service', 'poor', 'Active-active writes for everything is the most expensive and complex option, especially for data (conflicts, consistency). Most of the business may not need it, and next quarter is unrealistic.'],
    ['Pin down the real requirement: recovery time and data-loss tolerance per capability, budget, and data-residency rules', 'best', 'Yes. “Multi-region” covers everything from backups in another region to full active-active. Agreeing RTO (how fast we must recover) and RPO (how much data we can lose) per capability turns a slogan into a design.'],
    ['Switch cloud providers', 'poor', 'Every provider has regional outages. Switching costs a lot and doesn’t add a second region.'],
    ['Add a fourth availability zone', 'meh', 'Zones protect against data-centre failures, which you already handle. A regional outage takes out all zones in the region.']],
  walk:{
    diagnose:['<b>RTO and RPO per capability</b>: checkout (RPO ≈ 0, RTO ≤ 30 min?), browsing (stale is fine, RTO minutes), back office (hours)', '<b>What failed last week</b>: compute, the database, DNS, or a dependency (identity, payments) that was single-region too?', '<b>Budget</b>: a second region costs roughly from 10–20% (pilot light) to ~100%+ (full active-active) more', '<b>Data</b>: asynchronous replication means a little loss on failover; synchronous cross-region replication adds latency to every write', '<b>Data residency</b>: may customer data leave the country? (In India, regions such as Mumbai and Hyderabad stay in-country.)'],
    options:[['Backup and restore in another region', 'Cheap', 'Recovery takes hours', 'Minimum for everything'], ['Pilot light (data replicated, compute off)', 'Low cost; RPO seconds', 'RTO an hour or more to scale up', 'Back office'], ['Warm standby (scaled-down copy running)', 'RTO in minutes; RPO seconds', 'Pay for a second, smaller stack', 'Checkout'], ['Active-active reads, single write region', 'Browsing survives regional loss', 'Writes still fail over', 'Browse and catalogue'], ['Full active-active writes', 'Near-zero RTO', 'Conflict handling or partitioning by home region; highest cost', 'Not now']],
    decision:'Tier the design by capability rather than making everything active-active. Browsing and catalogue become <b>active-active for reads</b> (stateless services in two regions, replicated read data and the CDN). Checkout and payments get a <b>warm standby</b> in a second in-country region with asynchronous database replication (RPO of seconds) and a practised failover with an RTO under 30 minutes. Backups are copied cross-region immediately. Full active-active writes are reconsidered only if the business needs an RTO near zero for checkout.',
    plan:[['Weeks 0–2', 'Agree RTO/RPO per capability; copy backups cross-region; audit hidden single-region dependencies (identity, secrets, DNS, CI)', 'Signed-off targets; backups restorable in region B'], ['Weeks 2–8', 'Infrastructure as code for the whole stack; cross-region database replica; read services deployed in both regions', 'Region B can serve browsing'], ['Weeks 8–12', 'Warm standby for checkout; health-checked traffic management; failover runbook', 'First failover drill under 30 minutes'], ['Ongoing', 'Quarterly failover game days; replication lag alerts', 'Drills meet RTO/RPO']],
    risks:[['Replication lag means lost orders on failover', 'Alert on lag; reconcile from payment-provider records after failover'], ['Failover has never been tested', 'Quarterly drills, including failing back'], ['Hidden single-region dependency', 'Dependency audit; test with region A fully blocked'], ['Split brain: both regions accept writes', 'Fencing: the old primary is demoted before the new one takes writes']],
    measure:['RTO and RPO achieved in drills, per capability', 'Replication lag p99', 'Cost of the second region as a % of the bill', 'Error budget consumed by regional incidents'],
    say:'“Full active-active everywhere would take more than a year and double our costs, and most of the site doesn’t need it. Here’s what I propose: browsing keeps working through a regional outage, and checkout moves to a second Indian region within 30 minutes with at most a few seconds of data at risk. We’ll have it in about three months and prove it with a drill every quarter. If we later need zero-downtime checkout, we’ll have the foundations for it.”',
    real:[{co:'AWS', tier:'confirmed', text:'AWS’s disaster recovery guidance describes four strategies with rising cost and falling RTO/RPO: backup and restore, pilot light, warm standby and multi-site active-active.', src:'AWS whitepaper, “Disaster Recovery of Workloads on AWS”'}, {co:'Netflix', tier:'confirmed', text:'Netflix has described running active-active across AWS regions and regularly evacuating a region to prove it can.', src:'Netflix Technology Blog'}]},
  after:{n:[['u', 'client', 'Shoppers', '', 0, 1], ['cdn', 'cdn', 'CDN', 'catalogue, images', 1, 0], ['dns', 'lb', 'GeoDNS', 'health-checked', 1, 1], ['appM', 'service', 'Mumbai', 'active', 2, 1], ['appH', 'service', 'Hyderabad', 'warm standby', 2, 2], ['rd', 'cache', 'Catalogue replicas', 'both regions', 1, 2], ['dbM', 'db', 'Primary', 'Mumbai', 3, 1], ['dbH', 'db', 'Replica', 'async, RPO s', 3, 2]], e:'u>cdn u>dns dns>appM dns>appH appM>dbM appH>dbH dbM>dbH appM>rd appH>rd', f:[['Normal day: browsing is served from the CDN and catalogue replicas; checkout writes go to the Mumbai primary and replicate to Hyderabad.', 'u>dns|dns>appM|appM>dbM:checkout write|dbM>dbH:async replication'], ['Mumbai fails: health checks move traffic, the replica is promoted, and checkout resumes in Hyderabad.', 'u>dns|dns>appH:failover|appH>dbH:promoted to primary']]},
  adr:{title:'Tiered multi-region: active-active reads, warm standby for checkout', context:'A four-hour regional outage took the site down and consumed ~46% of the yearly 99.9% error budget. Everything runs in one region; backups are stored in the same region. Leadership asked for active-active multi-region.', drivers:'Survive a full regional outage\nCheckout data loss at most seconds; recovery under 30 minutes\nKeep data in India\nCost proportional to business need', options:'Backup and restore only\nPilot light\nWarm standby for checkout + active-active reads for browsing\nFull active-active writes', decision:'Browsing and catalogue run active-active for reads in two in-country regions. Checkout and payments run a warm standby with asynchronous replication and a practised failover (RTO < 30 min, RPO seconds). Backups are copied cross-region immediately. Failover is drilled quarterly.', positive:'Survives regional loss at a fraction of active-active cost\nFoundations (IaC, replication, drills) enable more later', negative:'Seconds of checkout data at risk on failover\nSecond region adds roughly 25–40% to infrastructure cost (estimate)', revisit:'Business requires near-zero RTO for checkout, or regional incidents recur'},
  refs:['l:multi-az-region', 'l:disaster-recovery', 'l:redundancy-failover', 'l:replication', 'l:availability-nines', 'l:geodns-anycast', 'l:cap-pacelc']});

/* 8 ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
S({id:'five-year', icon:'🔭', title:'Design for the next five years', tags:['evolutionary architecture', 'one-way doors', 'fitness functions'],
  tagline:'A new lending platform must grow 10× and add products and countries. Leadership asks for a “future-proof” architecture.',
  situation:`<p>You are setting the architecture for a new digital lending platform. In five years the business expects about 10× more customers, three more loan products, and launches in two more countries with their own regulators. Leadership asks for an architecture that is “future-proof” and “won’t need a rewrite”.</p>`,
  facts:['Launch: one country, one product, ~200K customers in year one', 'Plan: 10× customers, 3 more products, 2 more countries in 5 years', 'Strict regulation: audit trails, data residency per country', 'Team: 25 engineers now, maybe 100 later', 'Unknowns: which products succeed, which countries come first'],
  ask:'What is your first move?',
  choices:[
    ['Pick the most scalable stack now: microservices, Kafka, Cassandra, Kubernetes across regions', 'poor', 'This optimises for a scale you may never reach, in shapes you can’t predict, and slows the first launch. Most of these choices are easy to add later and expensive to carry early.'],
    ['Design every component for 10× from day one', 'meh', 'Some parts deserve it, but designing everything for 10× wastes effort on parts that will change anyway. Focus on what will be hard to change.'],
    ['Separate the decisions that are expensive to reverse from those that are cheap, and invest only in getting the expensive ones right', 'best', 'Yes. You can’t predict the future, but you can tell which decisions lock you in: the ledger and data model, public APIs, identity, how countries and tenants are isolated, audit. Get those right and keep everything else cheap to change.'],
    ['Defer architecture decisions until requirements are clear', 'poor', 'Requirements won’t be clear in lending until you launch. Deferring the one-way-door decisions (ledger model, data residency) means making them by accident.']],
  walk:{
    diagnose:['<b>One-way doors</b> (expensive to reverse): the ledger and money model, public API contracts, customer identity, country/tenant isolation and data residency, audit and event history', '<b>Two-way doors</b> (cheap to reverse): web framework, queue vendor, cache, internal service boundaries, deployment tooling', '<b>Likely change</b>: new products (different interest rules, schedules), new countries (regulation, currency, language), much larger volume', '<b>Unlikely change</b>: double-entry accounting, the need for audit, idempotent money movement', '<b>Constraints that compound</b>: regulators, data residency, and partner integrations that are slow to change'],
    options:[['Maximum-scale stack from day one', 'Theoretically ready for scale', 'Slow launch; complexity in the wrong places', 'No'], ['Simple monolith, no special care', 'Fastest launch', 'One-way doors decided by accident', 'No'], ['Simple core + careful one-way doors + fitness functions', 'Fast launch; safe evolution', 'Requires discipline and explicit decisions', 'Yes']],
    decision:'Build a modular monolith around clear domains (customers, applications, underwriting, loans, ledger, collections) and invest heavily in the one-way doors: a double-entry ledger with immutable entries; versioned public APIs; a product-rules layer so new loan products are configuration plus code modules, not forks; a country dimension in every data model with per-country data stores where residency demands it; and an append-only event history of business facts. Protect key qualities with fitness functions in CI and production (dependency rules, latency budgets, ledger balance checks, cost per loan). Write a “scale ladder” that names what changes at 2×, 5× and 10×.',
    plan:[['Months 0–3', 'Domain map; ledger and API design reviews; ADRs for each one-way door', 'Every one-way door has an ADR'], ['Months 3–9', 'Launch on the modular monolith; fitness functions in CI; event history from day one', 'Launch; fitness functions green'], ['Year 1–2', 'Second product via the rules layer; second country with its own data store', 'New product in < 1 quarter; new country without forking code'], ['Years 2–5', 'Extract services where the scale ladder triggers fire; revisit ADRs on their review dates', 'No rewrite needed']],
    risks:[['Over-engineering the “flexible” rules layer', 'Build it from the second product’s real needs, not imagined ones'], ['Fitness functions ignored when red', 'Treat failures like failing tests; owners per function'], ['Country isolation added late', 'Country id in every record from day one; per-country stores ready'], ['Team growth erodes boundaries', 'Ownership map; dependency checks; architecture reviews twice a year']],
    measure:['Time to launch a new product', 'Time to launch a new country', 'Fitness functions passing', 'Lead time staying flat as the team grows', 'Unit cost per active loan'],
    say:'“Nobody can design for a future we can’t see, so we won’t try to guess the technology. We’ll get the few decisions that are expensive to undo exactly right (how money is recorded, how countries are separated, our public APIs) and keep everything else simple and easy to change. Automated checks will tell us the moment we drift. That’s how we avoid a rewrite: not by predicting the future, but by keeping change cheap.”',
    real:[{co:'Building Evolutionary Architectures', tier:'confirmed', text:'Ford, Parsons and Kua describe fitness functions as automated checks that protect architectural characteristics as a system changes.', src:'Ford, Parsons & Kua, Building Evolutionary Architectures (2017)'}, {co:'Amazon', tier:'confirmed', text:'Bezos’s 2015 letter separates one-way-door decisions (made carefully) from two-way-door decisions (made quickly).', src:'Amazon 2015 letter to shareholders'}]},
  adr:{title:'Evolutionary architecture: protect one-way doors, keep everything else reversible', context:'A new lending platform launches in one country with one product; plans call for 10× customers, 3 more products and 2 more countries in five years, under strict regulation and data residency rules. Leadership asked for a “future-proof” architecture.', drivers:'Launch within 9 months\nAdd products and countries without forking or rewriting\nRegulatory audit and data residency\nTeam growth from 25 to ~100 engineers', options:'Maximum-scale distributed stack from day one\nSimple monolith with no special care\nModular monolith with carefully designed one-way doors and fitness functions', decision:'Modular monolith by domain. Invest in one-way doors: double-entry immutable ledger, versioned public APIs, product-rules layer, country dimension and per-country stores, append-only business events. Fitness functions in CI and production protect boundaries, latency, ledger balance and unit cost. A scale ladder defines what changes at 2×, 5× and 10×.', positive:'Fast first launch\nNew products and countries without forks\nEvolution guarded by automated checks', negative:'Upfront design effort on the ledger and country model\nFitness functions need ongoing ownership', revisit:'Each scale-ladder trigger, and every ADR on its review date'},
  refs:['l:tradeoff-thinking', 'l:ddd-bounded-contexts', 'l:api-design', 'l:hexagonal', 'l:event-driven', 'l:multi-tenancy', 'l:infrastructure-as-code']});
})();
